      "question": "Joaquin pawns his favorite watch for a loan of $120. After 30 days, he buys his watch back for $140. What interest did the pawnbroker charge Joaquin?",
      "answer": "The pawnbroker charged Joaquin an interest of $20. Joaquin pawned his watch for $120. This is the principal amount, which is the initial sum of money lent to Joaquin by the pawnbroker. After 30 days, Joaquin decided to buy back his watch for $140. This amount includes the principal amount plus the interest charged by the pawnbroker. To find out the interest charged, we subtract the principal amount from the total amount paid: Interest charged = Total amount paid - Principal amount Interest charged = $140 - $120 Interest charged = $20 Therefore, the pawnbroker charged Joaquin an interest of $20 for the 30-day loan.",
